      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
